<h2>Maintenance beyond the Hindu Marriage Act</h2>
<p>Indian matrimonial law is a federation of statutes. A Hindu spouse looks to the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955; a Christian spouse to the Divorce Act, 1869; a Parsi spouse to the Parsi Marriage and Divorce Act, 1936. Each of these personal statutes carries its own machinery for two kinds of financial support: <strong>alimony pendente lite</strong> — interim support while the matrimonial case is pending — and <strong>permanent alimony</strong>, fixed at or after the decree. Over and above all of them sit two religion-neutral remedies: the summary maintenance jurisdiction under Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 (formerly Section 125 CrPC), and monetary relief under the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005.</p>
<p>Understanding which statute applies, and how the remedies stack, is the first task in any maintenance brief involving a Christian or Parsi marriage.</p>

<h2>The Divorce Act, 1869: Sections 36 and 37</h2>
<p>The Divorce Act, 1869 governs divorce, judicial separation and allied reliefs for persons married under Christian law. <strong>Section 36</strong> provides for alimony pendente lite: while a suit under the Act is pending, the wife may apply for an order that the husband pay her alimony during the suit, and the court fixes a sum it considers just having regard to the husband’s income. The provision contemplates expeditious disposal of such applications. <strong>Section 37</strong> deals with permanent alimony: when a decree of dissolution or judicial separation is passed, the court may order the husband to secure to the wife, or pay her periodically, such gross sum or such monthly or periodical payment as it thinks reasonable, having regard to her own property, the husband’s ability and the conduct of the parties.</p>
<p>The Act was substantially modernised by the Indian Divorce (Amendment) Act, 2001, which liberalised the grounds and procedure under the statute. The working position today is that Christian spouses litigate these claims in the Family Court like any other matrimonial party, with quantum governed by the same disclosure-driven exercise described below.</p>

<h2>The Parsi Marriage and Divorce Act, 1936: Sections 39 and 40</h2>
<p>For Parsi marriages, <strong>Section 39</strong> of the Parsi Marriage and Divorce Act, 1936 provides for alimony pendente lite: where a suit under the Act is pending, a spouse who has no independent income sufficient for support and the necessary expenses of the suit may seek an order that the other spouse pay the expenses of the proceeding and a weekly or monthly sum during the suit, fixed on the parties’ respective incomes. <strong>Section 40</strong> provides for permanent alimony and maintenance: at the time of passing any decree or at any time subsequent to it, the court may order the respondent to pay the applicant a gross sum or periodical payments for maintenance, having regard to the respondent’s income and property, the applicant’s income and property, the conduct of the parties and other circumstances. The provision also permits variation of the order on change of circumstances — a structure that consciously parallels the modern matrimonial statutes.</p>
<p>Notably, these Parsi-law provisions are framed so that either spouse may apply — the statute speaks of the plaintiff or defendant, and of a spouse without sufficient independent income — making them available, in appropriate facts, to a husband as much as to a wife.</p>

<h2>How the statutes compare</h2>
