<thead><tr><th>Statute</th><th>Interim support</th><th>Permanent alimony</th><th>Who may apply</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Divorce Act, 1869 (Christian)</td><td>Section 36 — alimony pendente lite</td><td>Section 37 — gross sum or periodical payment</td><td>Wife</td></tr>
<tr><td>Parsi Marriage and Divorce Act, 1936</td><td>Section 39 — expenses and interim sum</td><td>Section 40 — gross sum or periodical payment, variable</td><td>Either spouse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hindu Marriage Act, 1955</td><td>Section 24 — pendente lite and expenses</td><td>Section 25 — permanent alimony</td><td>Either spouse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Special Marriage Act, 1954 (civil marriages)</td><td>Alimony pendente lite under the Act</td><td>Permanent alimony under the Act</td><td>Wife (interim); as the Act provides</td></tr>
<tr><td>Section 144 BNSS (religion-neutral)</td><td>Interim maintenance during the proceeding</td><td>Monthly allowance until altered or cancelled</td><td>Wife, children, parents</td></tr>
<tr><td>DV Act, 2005 (religion-neutral)</td><td>Interim monetary relief under Section 23</td><td>Monetary relief under Section 20</td><td>Aggrieved woman</td></tr>
<tr><td>Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986</td><td colspan="2">Distinct scheme, covered separately on this site</td><td>Divorced Muslim woman</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Interaction with Section 144 BNSS and the DV Act</h2>
<p>The personal statutes and the religion-neutral remedies are cumulative, not mutually exclusive. A Christian or Parsi wife may invoke Section 144 BNSS without any matrimonial suit at all — the provision requires only neglect or refusal to maintain and her inability to maintain herself. She may likewise seek monetary relief under Section 20 of the DV Act, which expressly extends to maintenance for the aggrieved woman and her children, in addition to an order under Section 144 BNSS. The discipline the courts apply is one of <em>adjustment</em>: amounts awarded in one proceeding are disclosed and set off in the other, so that the husband pays a fair total rather than overlapping sums, and the wife is not relegated from one forum to another.</p>

<h2>Forum and quantum: the common ground</h2>
<p>In Delhi, the Family Courts established under the Family Courts Act, 1984 hear suits under the Divorce Act and proceedings for maintenance, including applications under Section 144 BNSS; DV Act applications go before the Magistrate. Whatever the statute, quantum is decided on the same practical footing: sworn affidavits of income, assets, expenditure and liabilities from both sides; the standard of living enjoyed during the marriage; the earning capacity and actual earnings of each spouse; the needs of dependent children; and the conduct and duration of the marriage where the statute makes conduct relevant. Interim figures lean on disclosed income; permanent alimony weighs the longer horizon, including a lump-sum settlement where a clean break is sensible.</p>

<div class="note"><p>Parties to Christian and Parsi marriages sometimes assume that maintenance law offers them less than it offers Hindu spouses. The assumption is mistaken: the 1869 and 1936 statutes contain full interim and permanent alimony machinery, and the BNSS and DV Act apply to every community. The real work lies in choosing the right combination of forums and proving income honestly and early.</p></div>
